 dict(title="Spot the common factor",rules=["\\text{Factor }a(x)\\text{ and }b(x)","x=c\\text{ is a zero of }b:\\ \\text{hole or VA?}"],
  examples=[dict(q="r(x)=\\dfrac{x^{2}-4}{x^{2}-2x}",steps=["r(x)=\\dfrac{(x-2)(x+2)}{x(x-2)}","\\text{hole at }x=2,\\ \\text{VA }x=0","\\text{hole }(2,2),\\ \\text{zero }x=-2"]),
            dict(q="r(x)=\\dfrac{x^{2}-2x-3}{x^{2}-x-6}",steps=["r(x)=\\dfrac{(x-3)(x+1)}{(x-3)(x+2)}","\\text{hole at }x=3,\\ \\text{VA }x=-2","\\text{hole }\\left(3,\\dfrac{4}{5}\\right),\\ \\text{zero }x=-1"])],fig=None,
  notes="Speaker notes (Targeted Instruction, block 1 of 3, LO 1.10.A). LO 1.10.A: 'Determine holes in graphs of rational functions.' Suggested skills focus 3.C: 'Support conclusions or choices with a logical rationale or appropriate data.'\nWorked solutions: Example A: x^2 - 4 = (x - 2)(x + 2) and x^2 - 2x = x(x - 2). The factor x - 2 is in both, so there is a hole at x = 2; the reduced function (x + 2)/x has value 4/2 = 2 there, so the hole is (2, 2). The factor x stays in the denominator: vertical asymptote x = 0. The zero is x = -2. Example B: x^2 - 2x - 3 = (x - 3)(x + 1) and x^2 - x - 6 = (x - 3)(x + 2). Hole at x = 3; reduced value (3 + 1)/(3 + 2) = 4/5, so the hole is (3, 4/5). The factor x + 2 remains: vertical asymptote x = -2. The zero is x = -1.\nVerbal misconceptions: 'every zero of the denominator is a vertical asymptote'; 'a factor that cancels leaves no trace on the graph'; 'the hole is on the x-axis because the numerator is zero there' (the numerator of the reduced function is what matters).\nAdaptive routing: if more than 30% miss Gate Q1, return to this slide and redo Example A aloud."),
 dict(title="Compare the multiplicities",rules=["m_{\\text{num}}\\ge m_{\\text{den}}\\Rightarrow\\text{hole at }x=c","m_{\\text{num}}<m_{\\text{den}}\\Rightarrow\\text{VA at }x=c"],
  examples=[dict(q="r(x)=\\dfrac{(x-1)^{2}(x+2)}{(x-1)(x+3)}",steps=["m_{\\text{num}}=2\\ge m_{\\text{den}}=1","\\text{hole at }x=1,\\ \\text{VA }x=-3","\\text{simplified: }\\dfrac{(x-1)(x+2)}{x+3}\\Rightarrow(1,0)"]),
            dict(q="r(x)=\\dfrac{x+1}{(x+1)^{2}(x-4)}",steps=["m_{\\text{num}}=1<m_{\\text{den}}=2","\\text{no hole; VA }x=-1\\text{ and }x=4","\\text{simplified: }\\dfrac{1}{(x+1)(x-4)}"])],fig=None,
  notes="Speaker notes (Targeted Instruction, block 2 of 3, LO 1.10.A). EK 1.10.A.1: 'If the multiplicity of a real zero in the numerator is greater than or equal to its multiplicity in the denominator, then the graph of the rational function has a hole at the corresponding input value.' The second rule on the slide (less than gives a vertical asymptote) is the contrast case, not a quotation from the CED.\nWorked solutions: Example A: at x = 1 the numerator has multiplicity 2 and the denominator multiplicity 1; 2 >= 1, so there is a hole at x = 1. After cancelling one factor, (x - 1)(x + 2)/(x + 3) is still zero at x = 1, so the hole is the point (1, 0). The factor x + 3 gives the vertical asymptote x = -3, and the remaining zero is x = -2. Example B: at x = -1 the numerator has multiplicity 1 and the denominator multiplicity 2; 1 < 2, so no hole. After cancelling, 1/((x + 1)(x - 4)) is undefined at x = -1: vertical asymptote x = -1. The factor x - 4 gives a second asymptote x = 4.\nVerbal misconceptions: 'a hole always has y-value not zero'; 'cancelling the factor once always removes the zero or the asymptote completely'; 'the factor in the numerator cancels so there is a hole' (compare the powers first).\nAdaptive routing: if more than 30% miss Gate Q2, return to this slide and redo Example B with the multiplicity count written under each factor."),
